{"source_type":"article","title":"Religion and the Aramaic Incantation Bowls","authors":"Michael G. Morony","issued_year":2007,"container_title":"Religion Compass","doi":"10.1111/j.1749-8171.2007.00029.x","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1749-8171.2007.00029.x","citation":"Morony, Michael G. 'Religion and the Aramaic Incantation Bowls.' Religion Compass 1 (2007). DOI: 10.1111/j.1749-8171.2007.00029.x.","access_status":"unknown","rights_status":"copyrighted","notes":"Argues for a plural religious environment; a basic statement against reading script as religion. Volume and pages need confirming."}
{"source_type":"book","title":"Rabbis, Sorcerers, Kings, and Priests: The Culture of the Talmud in Ancient Iran","authors":"Jason Sion Mokhtarian","issued_year":2015,"publisher":"University of California Press","url":"https://academic.oup.com/california-scholarship-online/book/13276/chapter/166607539","citation":"Mokhtarian, Jason Sion. Rabbis, Sorcerers, Kings, and Priests: The Culture of the Talmud in Ancient Iran. Berkeley: University of California Press, 2015.","access_status":"partial","rights_status":"copyrighted","notes":"Major bowl-Talmud comparative synthesis that resists derivation in either direction. Co-editor of the 2025 field volume."}
